version: 2
title: How a version-1 song works
description: Clips placed on tracks — an arrangement that reads its clips live, draws the tracks against the bars, opens a clip on click, and exports one multi-track MIDI file for Ableton Live.
decisions:
  - key: host
    label: Where does an export land?
    values:
      - key: store
        label: A host with a binary writer
        detail: The desktop app, VS Code, the web Studio — the file is written beside the song.
      - key: bare
        label: A host without one
        detail: The bytes are offered as a browser download.
events:
  - key: always
    label: Always
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Always
    detail: What holds at every moment — the shape of the file, how bars and placements are counted, and where the engine lives.
    content:
      key: shape
      label: The shape of the file
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: The shape of the file
        description: Tracks of placements; every clip is a file beside the song.
        sections:
          - title: Top-level keys
            body: |
              | key | what it is | default |
              |---|---|---|
              | `title` | the heading | none |
              | `tempo` | beats per minute | the first clip's, else 120 |
              | `time` | the time signature | the first clip's, else 4/4 |
              | `tracks` | the arrangement — one MIDI track each on export | |
          - title: A track and its placements
            body: |
              ```yaml
              tracks:
                - name: Drums
                  channel: 10                                 # optional; the first clip's own otherwise
                  clips:
                    - {file: drums.clip, at: 0, repeat: 8}    # from bar 0, eight times back to back
                    - {file: fill.clip, at: 7}
                - name: Bass
                  clips:
                    - {file: bass.clip, repeat: 4}            # no `at` = right after the previous clip on this track
                    - {file: bass.clip, repeat: 4, transpose: 5}
              ```

              Bars count from 0 at the song's start, in the song's time signature. `at` is where a
              placement begins (default: where the previous one on the track ended, or 0), `repeat`
              how many times the clip plays back to back (default 1), `transpose` semitones added
              to every pitch (default 0). Clip refs are relative to the song's folder. A track with
              no `name` is "Track N".
          - title: Where the engine lives
            body: |
              `packages/filekinds/src/lib/songDoc.ts` — `parseSong`, `loadSong`, `songMidiSpec`,
              `songSummary`; its header comment is what `studio-check --spec song` prints, with
              `clipDoc.ts` and `midiFile.ts` beside it. The view is `components/music/SongView.tsx`
              over `PianoRoll.tsx` and `ExportMidiButton.tsx`; the checker is `apps/cli/src/check.ts`.
  - key: opened
    label: The file is opened
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Opening
    detail: parseSong reads the YAML once and never throws; then every clip is read from the store and the arrangement is laid out in beats.
    content:
      key: load
      label: What is parsed, what is read, and what is drawn
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: What is parsed, what is read, and what is drawn
        description: A lenient parse, a live read of the clips, then the arrangement.
        sections:
          - title: The parse
            body: |
              A YAML error is the first problem and the song is otherwise empty. Named problems:
              `tempo: x is not a bpm`, `time: x is not a time signature like 4/4`, `tracks: must be
              a list`, `track <name>: channel x is not 1–16`, `track <name>: clips must be a list`,
              and per placement `track <name>, clip N: no file` (the placement is dropped), `at x
              is not a bar` (a number, at least 0), `repeat x is not a count` (a whole number, at
              least 1), `transpose x is not a number of semitones`. A bad value falls back to its
              default; the rest of the song still renders and exports.
          - title: Reading the clips
            body: |
              "Loading the clips…" while `loadSong` reads each placement's file through the store,
              resolved against the song's folder, once per path. A clip that cannot be read is
              `track <name>: x.clip is not there`. The song's tempo and time signature are its own,
              else the first clip read, else 120 and 4/4. Per track a cursor runs: a placement
              starts at `at` × beats per bar, else at the cursor; it lasts the clip's bars (in the
              clip's own signature; one bar for a missing clip) times `repeat`, and the cursor
              moves to its end. The song's length is the last end, rounded up to a bar. A track's
              channel is its own, else its first clip's, else 1. Every pass of every clip
              contributes its notes, shifted and transposed; a pitch pushed outside 0–127 is
              dropped. A clip with problems adds `track <name>: x.clip has N problems of its own`.
          - title: What is drawn
            body: |
              The title and `N tracks · N bars · N notes · N bpm · 4/4`, with Export .mid (disabled
              when no track has a note). The problems in red. "No tracks yet — give it `tracks:`
              with `clips:`." when there are none. Then the arrangement: a gutter with each track's
              colour, name and note count; a ruler numbering the bars (every fourth, or every one
              when there is room); on each track a block per placement, as long as it plays,
              labelled `<stem> ×N +T`, its repeats marked off with dashed lines, a thumbnail of its
              notes inside, and a dashed red block reading `x.clip — not there` for a missing
              clip. A short song stretches to fill the pane, at no less than ten pixels a beat.
  - key: block-clicked
    label: A placement is clicked
    trigger: chosen
    arity: many
    domain: Reading
    detail: The host opens the clip file.
    content:
      key: open
      label: What opens
      kind: md
      doc: |
        The block hands the clip's absolute path to the host's `onOpenPath` — the Studio
        navigates to the clip, and its roll opens — so the song is a map of its clips. A host
        that passes no `onOpenPath` draws the block without a pointer and a click does nothing.
        Hovering a block says `x.clip · bar N for N bars · transposed T — open it`.
  - key: track-clicked
    label: A track's name is clicked
    trigger: chosen
    arity: many
    domain: Reading
    detail: That track's every note, across the whole song, on a piano roll below.
    content:
      key: roll
      label: What the roll shows
      kind: md
      doc: |
        A line reads `<name> · channel N · N notes`, then a piano roll over the song's bars with
        every note the track plays — every pass of every placement, already transposed — in the
        track's colour, rows labelled in the naming of the first clip that could be read, lane
        names carried from the clips (at their transposed pitches). Clicking the name again closes
        it. The roll edits nothing.
  - key: exported
    label: Export .mid is clicked
    trigger: chosen
    arity: many
    domain: Exporting
    detail: The whole song as one format 1 Standard MIDI File — one named track per song track.
    hint: Say what the host has — the same bytes go to the store or to a download.
    content:
      key: export
      label: What is written, and where
      kind: md
      by: [host]
      docs:
        host=store: |
          `songMidiSpec` gives `writeMidiFile` a format 1 file: track 0 holds the tempo, the time
          signature and the title (the song's, else the file's stem); then one track per song
          track, named, on its channel, with every note the arrangement gave it, at 480 ticks per
          quarter, note-offs before note-ons at one tick. Dropped on Ableton Live's Arrangement
          that is one named track per MIDI track at the song's tempo.

          The bytes go through the host's binary writer to `<stem>.mid` in the song's folder,
          overwriting the last export. "wrote <stem>.mid" shows for four seconds; an error shows
          its message in red.
        host=bare: |
          The same format 1 bytes — track 0 with tempo, time signature and title, then one named
          track per song track on its channel — offered as a download named `<stem>.mid`, since
          the host has no writer to reach the store. "wrote <stem>.mid" shows for four seconds
          either way.
  - key: cli-exported
    label: studio-check --midi runs on it
    trigger: chosen
    domain: Exporting
    detail: The same bytes from the command line, the clips read from disk.
    content:
      key: cli
      label: What the command does
      kind: md
      doc: |
        `studio-check --midi x.song [name.mid]` parses the song, reads its clips from the disk
        beside it, prints every problem to stderr, writes `x.mid` (or the name given) in the
        song's folder and prints `x.mid: <summary> (N bytes)`. The exit status is 1 whenever there
        were problems — a missing clip among them — even though the file was written.
  - key: changed-on-disk
    label: The file changes on disk
    trigger: imposed
    arity: many
    domain: Reading
    detail: The host re-reads; the song re-parses and reads its clips again — but only when this file changed.
    content:
      key: reread
      label: What is re-read
      kind: md
      doc: |
        The desktop watcher (250 ms debounce, `studio:fs-changed`), the web folder entry's server
        events or VS Code's text document deliver the new text, and the Studio re-reads unless its
        autosave is dirty, saving or in error. The song re-parses and `loadSong` runs again over
        every clip. A CLIP edited while the song sits open is not re-read until the song's own
        text or path changes, or it is reopened. The selected track stays selected by index.
  - key: checked
    label: studio-check runs on it
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Writing
    detail: One file in, one verdict out — on the command line the clips are read too.
    content:
      key: checker
      label: What the checker does
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: What the checker does
        description: Two checks that differ by what they can reach.
        sections:
          - title: On the command line
            body: |
              1. Parse the YAML — a parse error is the only problem reported.
              2. `parseSong`, then `loadSong` with the clips read from disk beside the file — so a
                 missing clip and a clip's own problems are problems here.

              The summary is the view's line: `N tracks · N bars · N notes · N bpm · 4/4`.
          - title: On the page
            body: |
              `parseSong` only. The summary is `N tracks, N clips`, and the clips are named under
              notes: `clips not read — this check has no folder: a.clip, b.clip`.
          - title: Not checked
            body: |
              Overlaps between placements on one track (they simply sound together). A `transpose`
              that pushes every note out of range (the notes are dropped, quietly). Whether the
              clips' signatures agree with the song's.
  - key: authored
    label: A song is written
    trigger: chosen
    domain: Writing
    content:
      key: write
      label: Writing one
      kind: guide
      doc:
        title: Write a song
        description: From the template to an arrangement — the clips first, then the placements.
        steps:
          - key: clips
            label: Have the clips
            detail: |
              One `.clip` per part in the song's folder, each checked. The song takes its tempo
              and signature from the first clip it reads when it gives none.
          - key: template
            label: Start from the template
            after: [clips]
            detail: |
              `studio-check --template song > new.song`:

              ```yaml
              title: new
              tracks:
                - name: Track 1
                  clips:
                    - {file: new.clip, at: 0, repeat: 4}
              ```
          - key: tracks
            label: Lay out the tracks
            after: [template]
            detail: |
              One track per instrument, `channel` where the DAW needs one (drums on 10). Place
              clips with `at` in bars, or leave it off to chain them; `repeat` for loops,
              `transpose` for the same riff elsewhere.
          - key: check
            label: Check it, then export
            after: [tracks]
            detail: |
              `studio-check new.song` reads the clips and prints the summary and every problem.
              Open it and press Export .mid, or `studio-check --midi new.song`, then drop `new.mid`
              on Ableton Live's Arrangement.
